Changing only the registration increased the number of clear removals recognized after camera motion in both engines. On the tabletop, this reproduced the direction of the exploratory result with two different target objects. Yet the new shelf errors expose what a successful registration leaves unresolved. The map can align a panel over the remembered place. The appearance rule can then read a poor object match and a low obstruction score as evidence for removal. On the certified MuJoCo pair, the delivered input alone could not distinguish the two equally represented states.

For a memory system with a cost for deleting a retained object, the clear-removal gain alone gives an incomplete basis for choosing a verifier. Each method's operating point has two consequences here. It determines how often the system makes a useful update when a change is visible and how often it commits when physical alternatives give it exactly the same evidence. The false removal with the blocker parked also shows why an audit needs to retain ordinary clear controls even when its main scientific question concerns occlusion.

For the tested language-model reader, most false deletions occurred when the target remained completely exposed. Its few commitments on identical opposite-state pairs illustrate the cost of guessing after the geometry gate passes. The unchanged-image controls, target sizes and crop preparation limit what this comparison tells us about occlusion. A trained place verifier could use the same pairs once its checkpoint and input settings become available.
